Here's a hardening checklist for a Slurm GPU partition on Rocky Linux 9. Test each change on one node before rolling it out, and confirm option names against your Slurm and driver versions.

## 1. Device isolation (Slurm + cgroups)

- [ ] **slurm.conf:** Use cgroup-based tracking and task plugins.
  ```
  ProctrackType=proctrack/cgroup
  TaskPlugin=task/cgroup,task/affinity
  GresTypes=gpu
  PrologFlags=Contain
  ```
- [ ] **cgroup.conf:** Constrain devices, cores and memory so jobs only see their allocated GPUs.
  ```
  ConstrainDevices=yes
  ConstrainCores=yes
  ConstrainRAMSpace=yes
  ```
- [ ] **gres.conf:** Use `AutoDetect=nvml` so Slurm maps GPUs (including MIG slices) by hardware. Hand-written device paths can drift.
- [ ] **Verify:** Run a 1-GPU job on a multi-GPU node and confirm `nvidia-smi` inside it shows only one device.

## 2. Node access control

- [ ] **pam_slurm_adopt:** Add it to `/etc/pam.d/sshd` (account section) so users can only SSH to nodes where they have a running job. Their session gets adopted into the job's cgroup, so it inherits the GPU restriction. This needs `PrologFlags=Contain`.
- [ ] **Admin exemption:** Exempt admins via a `pam_access` rule placed before `pam_slurm_adopt`.

## 3. NVIDIA driver hardening

- [ ] **Driver repo:** Install from NVIDIA's Rocky 9 repo, then pin the version with `dnf versionlock` so unattended updates don't swap drivers mid-production.
- [ ] **Security bulletins:** Subscribe to NVIDIA's security bulletins and set a patch cadence. Driver CVEs come out several times a year, and Qualys should flag them too.
- [ ] **Module options:** In `/etc/modprobe.d/nvidia.conf`, add:
  ```
  options nvidia NVreg_RestrictProfilingToAdminUsers=1
  ```
  This blocks the performance-counter side channel that lets one user profile another user's GPU work.
- [ ] **Persistence daemon:** Enable `nvidia-persistenced` instead of setting persistence mode by hand, and run it as a non-root user.
- [ ] **SELinux:** Keep it enforcing. The NVIDIA driver works fine with it, so check for AVC denials rather than switching it off.

## 4. Job prolog/epilog

- [ ] **Stray processes:** In the epilog, check for leftover GPU processes with `nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps=pid --format=csv,noheader`. Kill any you find. If they won't die, drain the node with a reason so the next user doesn't inherit a dirty GPU.
- [ ] **GPU settings:** Reset application clocks and compute mode in case a job changed them.
- [ ] **Health check:** Run a quick check before returning the node, either `dcgmi diag -r 1` or NHC with GPU checks.
- [ ] **Sharing modes:** Set compute mode to `EXCLUSIVE_PROCESS` on shared nodes. Don't run MPS across different users.

## 5. MIG (if you have A100/H100-class GPUs)

- [ ] **Partitioning:** Use MIG instead of MPS or time-slicing for multi-user sharing. MIG gives hardware memory isolation, and the other two don't.
- [ ] **Persistence:** Manage MIG layouts with `nvidia-mig-parted` so they survive reboots, and let `AutoDetect=nvml` expose the slices as GRES.

## 6. Containers

- [ ] **Apptainer:** Set `allow setuid = no` in `apptainer.conf` and use unprivileged user-namespace mode. Users run GPU containers with `--nv`.
- [ ] **NVIDIA Container Toolkit:** If installed, keep it on the latest release. CVE-2024-0132 and CVE-2025-23266 were both container escapes.
- [ ] **Image sources:** Point users to an approved registry or a local cache of vetted NGC images instead of arbitrary Docker Hub pulls.

## 7. Monitoring and detection

- [ ] **Metrics:** Run `dcgm-exporter` on GPU nodes and scrape it from your existing Prometheus.
- [ ] **Idle-node alert:** Alert when GPU utilization is above about 10% on a node Slurm reports as idle. That's the classic cryptomining signal.
- [ ] **Xid errors:** Forward kernel logs centrally and alert on Xid codes. Treat 48, 63, 64, 79, 94 and 95 as high priority. Xid 79 means "fell off the bus," and 48 is a double-bit ECC error.
- [ ] **DCGM hostengine:** Bind `nv-hostengine` (port 5555) to localhost or the management VLAN only.

## 8. Fabric (InfiniBand/RoCE)

- [ ] **Partitioning:** Configure P_Keys in OpenSM or UFM to separate tenant traffic from storage traffic.
- [ ] **Subnet Manager:** Run it on dedicated, locked-down hosts, and restrict who can run `ibdiagnet` or modify SM config.
- [ ] **NVSwitch systems:** Keep `nvidia-fabricmanager` on the same version as the driver, with no external exposure.

## 9. Firmware and out-of-band

- [ ] **Firmware inventory:** Track GPU VBIOS and NVSwitch firmware versions alongside BIOS. Update them with NVIDIA's firmware bundles during maintenance windows.
- [ ] **BMC network:** Keep iLO/iDRAC on an isolated management network with current firmware. A compromised BMC owns the GPUs too.

## 10. Data hygiene

- [ ] **Permissions:** Default dataset and scratch directories to `0750` or group-based access, never world-readable.
- [ ] **Model files:** Encourage `safetensors` over pickle-based checkpoints, since pickle files can execute code when loaded.

